{"type": "step", "node_id": 28, "parent_node_id": 26, "role": "assistant", "content": "## Solution\n\nWe seek the last four digits of $\\left\\lfloor \\dfrac{149^{151} + 151^{149}}{22499} \\right\\rfloor$, i.e. $\\left\\lfloor \\dfrac{N}{22499}\\right\\rfloor \\bmod 10000$, where $N = 149^{151} + 151^{149}$.\n\n**Key observation.** $22499 = 149 \\times 151$.\n\n**Reduction formula.** For $q = \\lfloor N/m\\rfloor$, we have $q \\bmod k = \\left\\lfloor (N \\bmod mk)/m \\right\\rfloor$. So we need $N \\bmod (22499 \\cdot 10000) = N \\bmod 224990000$, computed via CRT with coprime moduli $149,\\ 151,\\ 10000$.\n\n### Step 1: $N \\bmod 149$\n\n$149^{151} \\equiv 0 \\pmod{149}$, and $151 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{149}$, so $151^{149} \\equiv 2^{149} \\pmod{149}$. By Fermat ($149$ prime), $2^{148}\\equiv 1$, hence $2^{149}\\equiv 2$.\n\n$$N \\equiv 2 \\pmod{149}.$$\n\n### Step 2: $N \\bmod 151$\n\n$151^{149}\\equiv 0\\pmod{151}$, and $149\\equiv -2\\pmod{151}$, so $149^{151}\\equiv (-2)^{151} = -2^{151}\\pmod{151}$. By Fermat, $2^{150}\\equiv 1$, so $2^{151}\\equiv 2$, giving $-2^{151}\\equiv -2\\equiv 149$.\n\n$$N \\equiv 149 \\pmod{151}.$$\n\n### Step 3: $N \\bmod 10000$ (via $16$ and $625$)\n\n**Mod 16:** $149\\equiv 5$, $\\operatorname{ord}_{16}(5)=4$, $151\\bmod 4=3$, so $5^{151}\\equiv 5^3\\equiv 13$. $151\\equiv 7$, $\\operatorname{ord}_{16}(7)=2$, $149\\bmod 2=1$, so $7^{149}\\equiv 7$. Thus $N\\equiv 13+7=20\\equiv 4\\pmod{16}$.\n\n**Mod 625:** $\\varphi(625)=500$. Compute:\n- $149^2\\equiv 326,\\ 149^4\\equiv 26,\\ 149^5\\equiv 124,\\ 149^{10}\\equiv 376,\\ 149^{20}\\equiv 126,\\ 149^{25}\\equiv 126\\cdot124=15624\\equiv 624\\equiv -1$.\n  So $149^{50}\\equiv 1$, hence $149^{150}\\equiv 1$, and $149^{151}\\equiv 149\\pmod{625}$.\n- $151^2\\equiv 301,\\ 151^4\\equiv 601,\\ 151^5\\equiv 126,\\ 151^{10}\\equiv 251,\\ 151^{20}\\equiv 501,\\ 151^{25}\\equiv 501\\cdot126=63126\\equiv 1$.\n  So $151^{149}=151^{125}\\cdot151^{24}\\equiv 151^{24}=151^{20}\\cdot151^4\\equiv 501\\cdot601=301101\\equiv 476\\pmod{625}$.\n\n$$N\\equiv 149+476=625\\equiv 0\\pmod{625}.$$\n\n**CRT ($16,625$):** $x\\equiv 0\\pmod{625}$, $x\\equiv 4\\pmod{16}$. $x=625k$, $625\\equiv 1\\pmod{16}$, so $k\\equiv 4\\pmod{16}$, $k=4$, $x=2500$.\n\n$$N\\equiv 2500\\pmod{10000}.$$\n\n### Step 4: Combine via CRT\n\n**Mod $22499$:** $x\\equiv 2\\pmod{149}$, $x\\equiv 149\\pmod{151}$. Set $x=149+151t$; then $151t\\equiv 2\\pmod{149}$, i.e. $2t\\equiv 2$, so $t\\equiv 1$, giving $x=300$.\n\n$$N\\equiv 300\\pmod{22499}.$$\n\n**Mod $224990000$:** $x=300+22499s\\equiv 2500\\pmod{10000}$, i.e. $2499s\\equiv 2200\\pmod{10000}$. The inverse of $2499\\bmod 10000$: from $1=625\\cdot10000-2501\\cdot2499$, we get $2499^{-1}\\equiv -2501\\equiv 7499$. Thus $s\\equiv 7499\\cdot 2200\\equiv 7800\\pmod{10000}$.\n\n$$x = 300 + 22499\\cdot 7800 = 300 + 175492200 = 175492500.$$\n\n### Step 5: Extract the answer\n\n$$\\left\\lfloor\\frac{175492500}{22499}\\right\\rfloor = 7800 \\quad(\\text{since } 22499\\cdot 7800 = 175492200,\\ \\text{remainder }300).$$\n\nThe last four digits of the master code are $\\boxed{7800}$.\n\n### PROOF COMPLETE", "thinking": "The problem asks for the last four digits of floor((149^151 + 151^149) / 22499) mod 10000.\n\nLet me check for answer leaks... none found.\n\nI need to compute (149^151 + 151^149) mod (22499 * 10000) to get the floor mod 10000. Wait, not exactly. Let me think.\n\nWe want floor((149^151 + 151^149) / 22499) mod 10000.\n\nLet N = 149^151 + 151^149. We want floor(N/22499) mod 10000.\n\nfloor(N/22499) mod 10000 = floor((N mod (22499*10000)) / 22499) ... not quite. Actually floor(N/m) mod k where we need N mod (m*k) gives us floor((N mod (m*k))/m). Let me verify: N = q*m + r, q = floor(N/m). q mod k: q = a*k + b. N = (a*k+b)*m + r = a*k*m + b*m + r. So N mod (k*m) = b*m + r. floor((N mod (k*m))/m) = floor((b*m+r)/m) = b = q mod k. Yes!\n\nSo I need N mod (22499 * 10000) = N mod 224990000.\n\nLet me factor 224990000. 22499 * 10000. 10000 = 2^4 * 5^4. 22499 = ? Let me factor 22499. 22499 / 7 = 3214.14... no. /11 = 2045.36 no. /13 = 1730.69 no. /17 = 1323.47 no. /19 = 1184.16 no. /23 = 978.2 no. /29 = 775.8 no. /31 = 725.8 no. /37 = 608.1 no. /41 = 548.7 no. /43 = 523.2 no. /47 = 478.7 no. /53 = 424.5 no. /59 = 381.3 no. /61 = 368.8 no. /67 = 335.8 no. /71 = 316.9 no. /73 = 308.2 no. /79 = 284.8 no. /83 = 271.07 no. /89 = 252.8 no. /97 = 231.9 no. /101 = 222.7 no. /103 = 218.4 no. /107 = 210.3 no. /109 = 206.4 no. /113 = 199.1 no. sqrt(22499) ≈ 149.997. So check up to 149. /127 = 177.2 no. /131 = 171.7 no. /137 = 164.2 no. /139 = 161.9 no. /149 = 151.0. 149*151 = 22499! Yes! 149*151 = 22499.\n\nSo 22499 = 149 * 151.\n\nSo we need N = 149^151 + 151^149 mod (149*151*10000).\n\nThis is great because 149^151 is divisible by 149, and 151^149 is divisible by 151.\n\nLet me use CRT. M = 149 * 151 * 10000. Note gcd(149,151)=1, gcd(149,10000)=1 (149 prime, not 2 or 5), gcd(151,10000)=1.\n\nSo we need N mod M via CRT on moduli 149, 151, 10000.\n\nN mod 149: 149^151 ≡ 0 mod 149. 151 ≡ 2 mod 149. So 151^149 ≡ 2^149 mod 149. By Fermat, 2^148 ≡ 1 mod 149, so 2^149 ≡ 2 mod 149. So N ≡ 0 + 2 = 2 mod 149.\n\nN mod 151: 151^149 ≡ 0 mod 151. 149 ≡ -2 mod 151. 149^151 ≡ (-2)^151 = -2^151 mod 151. By Fermat 2^150 ≡ 1 mod 151, so 2^151 ≡ 2 mod 151. So (-2)^151 = -2^151 ≡ -2 mod 151. So N ≡ -2 mod 151 = 149 mod 151.\n\nN mod 10000: Need 149^151 + 151^149 mod 10000.\n\nLet me compute 149^151 mod 10000 and 151^149 mod 10000.\n\n149 mod 10000 = 149. Let's compute 149^151 mod 10000.\n\nUse CRT on 16 and 625 (since 10000 = 16*625).\n\n149 mod 16 = 149 - 144 = 5. 149^151 mod 16. 5^1=5, 5^2=25≡9, 5^3=45≡13, 5^4=65≡1 mod 16. So order 4. 151 mod 4 = 3. 5^151 ≡ 5^3 = 125 ≡ 13 mod 16. Wait 5^4 ≡ 1, 151 = 4*37 + 3, so 5^151 ≡ 5^3 = 125 ≡ 125 - 112 = 13 mod 16.\n\n151 mod 16 = 151 - 144 = 7. 7^1=7, 7^2=49≡1 mod 16. Order 2. 149 mod 2 = 1. 7^149 ≡ 7^1 = 7 mod 16.\n\nSo N mod 16 = 13 + 7 = 20 ≡ 4 mod 16.\n\nNow mod 625. 149 mod 625 = 149. 151 mod 625 = 151.\n\nCompute 149^151 mod 625 and 151^149 mod 625.\n\nφ(625) = 625 * 4/5 = 500. So by Euler, a^500 ≡ 1 mod 625 for gcd(a,625)=1. 149: gcd(149,625)=1. 151: gcd(151,625)=1.\n\n149^151 mod 625: 151 = 500*0 + 151. So need 149^151 mod 625.\n\n151^149 mod 625: 149 = 500*0 + 149. Need 151^149 mod 625.\n\nLet me compute these by repeated squaring mod 625.\n\n149^151 mod 625:\n149^1 = 149.\n149^2 = 22201. 22201 mod 625: 625*35 = 21875. 22201-21875 = 326. So 149^2 ≡ 326.\n149^4 = 326^2 = 106276. 625*170 = 106250. 106276-106250 = 26. So 149^4 ≡ 26.\n149^8 = 26^2 = 676. 676-625 = 51. So 149^8 ≡ 51.\n149^16 = 51^2 = 2601. 625*4 = 2500. 2601-2500 = 101. So 149^16 ≡ 101.\n149^32 = 101^2 = 10201. 625*16 = 10000. 10201-10000 = 201. So 149^32 ≡ 201.\n149^64 = 201^2 = 40401. 625*64 = 40000. 40401-40000 = 401. So 149^64 ≡ 401.\n149^128 = 401^2 = 160801. 625*257 = 160625. 160801-160625 = 176. So 149^128 ≡ 176.\n\n151 = 128 + 16 + 4 + 2 + 1. Check: 128+16=144, +4=148, +2=150, +1=151. Yes.\n\n149^151 = 149^128 * 149^16 * 149^4 * 149^2 * 149^1 mod 625\n= 176 * 101 * 26 * 326 * 149 mod 625.\n\nLet me compute step by step.\n176 * 101 = 17776. 625*28 = 17500. 17776-17500 = 276. So ≡ 276.\n276 * 26 = 7176. 625*11 = 6875. 7176-6875 = 301. So ≡ 301.\n301 * 326 = 98126. 625*156 = 97500. 98126-97500 = 626. 626-625 = 1. So ≡ 1.\n1 * 149 = 149. So 149^151 ≡ 149 mod 625.\n\nInteresting! Let me double check. 301*326: 301*326 = 301*300 + 301*26 = 90300 + 7826 = 98126. 98126 / 625 = 156.99..., 625*156 = 97500, 98126-97500=626, 626 mod 625 = 1. Yes. So 149^151 ≡ 149 mod 625.\n\nNow 151^149 mod 625:\n151^1 = 151.\n151^2 = 22801. 625*36 = 22500. 22801-22500 = 301. So 151^2 ≡ 301.\n151^4 = 301^2 = 90601. 625*144 = 90000. 90601-90000 = 601. So 151^4 ≡ 601.\n151^8 = 601^2 = 361201. 625*577 = 360625. 361201-360625 = 576. So 151^8 ≡ 576.\n151^16 = 576^2 = 331776. 625*530 = 331250. 331776-331250 = 526. So 151^16 ≡ 526.\n151^32 = 526^2 = 276676. 625*442 = 276250. 276676-276250 = 426. So 151^32 ≡ 426.\n151^64 = 426^2 = 181476. 625*290 = 181250. 181476-181250 = 226. So 151^64 ≡ 226.\n151^128 = 226^2 = 51076. 625*81 = 50625. 51076-50625 = 451. So 151^128 ≡ 451.\n\n149 = 128 + 16 + 4 + 1. Check: 128+16=144, +4=148, +1=149. Yes.\n\n151^149 = 151^128 * 151^16 * 151^4 * 151^1 mod 625\n= 451 * 526 * 601 * 151 mod 625.\n\n451 * 526 = 237226. 625*379 = 236875. 237226-236875 = 351. So ≡ 351.\n351 * 601 = 210951. 625*337 = 210625. 210951-210625 = 326. So ≡ 326.\n326 * 151 = 49226. 625*78 = 48750. 49226-48750 = 476. So ≡ 476.\n\nSo 151^149 ≡ 476 mod 625.\n\nN mod 625 = 149 + 476 = 625 ≡ 0 mod 625.\n\nSo N mod 10000: N ≡ 4 mod 16, N ≡ 0 mod 625.\n\nCRT: find x mod 10000 with x ≡ 0 mod 625 and x ≡ 4 mod 16.\nx = 625k. 625k ≡ 4 mod 16. 625 mod 16 = 625 - 624 = 1. So k ≡ 4 mod 16. k = 4. x = 2500.\nCheck: 2500 mod 625 = 0. 2500 mod 16 = 2500 - 2496 = 4. Yes.\n\nSo N mod 10000 = 2500.\n\nNow CRT to combine: N mod 149 = 2, N mod 151 = 149, N mod 10000 = 2500.\n\nWe want N mod M where M = 149*151*10000 = 22499*10000 = 224990000.\n\nFirst combine mod 149 and mod 151: N mod (149*151) = N mod 22499.\nN ≡ 2 mod 149, N ≡ 149 mod 151.\nx = 149 + 151*t. 149 + 151t ≡ 2 mod 149. 149 ≡ 0, so 151t ≡ 2 mod 149. 151 mod 149 = 2. So 2t ≡ 2 mod 149, t ≡ 1 mod 149. t = 1. x = 149 + 151 = 300.\nCheck: 300 mod 149 = 300-298 = 2. 300 mod 151 = 300-151 = 149. Yes.\n\nSo N ≡ 300 mod 22499.\n\nNow combine N ≡ 300 mod 22499 and N ≡ 2500 mod 10000.\nx = 300 + 22499*s. x ≡ 2500 mod 10000. 22499 mod 10000 = 2499. So 300 + 2499*s ≡ 2500 mod 10000. 2499*s ≡ 2200 mod 10000.\n\nNeed to solve 2499*s ≡ 2200 mod 10000. gcd(2499, 10000) = 1 (2499 = 3*833, 833 = 7*119 = 7*7*17, so 2499 = 3*7*7*17; 10000 = 2^4*5^4; coprime). So unique solution.\n\nFind inverse of 2499 mod 10000. Use extended Euclidean.\n10000 = 4*2499 + 4. 10000 - 4*2499 = 10000 - 9996 = 4.\n2499 = 624*4 + 3. 2499 - 624*4 = 2499 - 2496 = 3.\n4 = 1*3 + 1.\n3 = 3*1.\nBack-substitute: 1 = 4 - 1*3 = 4 - 1*(2499 - 624*4) = 625*4 - 2499 = 625*(10000 - 4*2499) - 2499 = 625*10000 - 2500*2499 - 2499 = 625*10000 - 2501*2499.\nSo -2501*2499 ≡ 1 mod 10000. Inverse of 2499 is -2501 mod 10000 = 7499.\n\nCheck: 2499*7499 mod 10000. 2499*7499 = 2499*7500 - 2499 = 18742500 - 2499 = 18740001. mod 10000 = 1. Yes!\n\ns ≡ 7499 * 2200 mod 10000. 7499*2200 = 7499*2000 + 7499*200 = 14998000 + 1499800 = 16497800. mod 10000 = 9800. Wait let me recompute: 14998000 mod 10000 = 8000. 1499800 mod 10000 = 9800. 8000+9800 = 17800 mod 10000 = 7800. Hmm let me just do 16497800 mod 10000 = 7800.\n\nSo s ≡ 7800 mod 10000. s = 7800.\n\nx = 300 + 22499*7800. 22499*7800 = 22499*78*100. 22499*78 = 22499*80 - 22499*2 = 1799920 - 44998 = 1754922. *100 = 175492200. x = 300 + 175492200 = 175492500.\n\nx mod 224990000: 175492500 < 224990000, so x = 175492500.\n\nSo N mod (22499*10000) = 175492500.\n\nfloor(N/22499) mod 10000 = floor(175492500 / 22499) mod 10000.\n\n175492500 / 22499: 22499 * 7800 = 175492200. 175492500 - 175492200 = 300. So floor = 7800, remainder 300.\n\nSo floor(N/22499) mod 10000 = 7800.\n\nWait, let me double-check the formula. We had N mod (22499*10000) = b*22499 + r where b = floor(N/22499) mod 10000 and r = N mod 22499. We found N mod 22499 = 300 (r=300) and N mod (22499*10000) = 175492500 = 7800*22499 + 300. So b = 7800.\n\nSo the answer is 7800.\n\nLet me verify the computation of N mod 10000 = 2500 once more, since that's the trickiest part.\n\nActually, let me verify 149^151 mod 625 = 149 more carefully. That's a strong claim (essentially 149^150 ≡ 1 mod 625).\n\nφ(625) = 500. 149^500 ≡ 1. But 149^150 ≡ 1 would require order dividing 150. Let me check the order of 149 mod 625.\n\nActually let me just verify 149^151 ≡ 149 by checking 149^150 ≡ 1 mod 625.\n\n149^2 ≡ 326, 149^4 ≡ 26, 149^5 = 149^4 * 149 = 26*149 = 3874. 3874 mod 625: 625*6=3750, 3874-3750=124. So 149^5 ≡ 124.\n149^10 = 124^2 = 15376. 625*24 = 15000. 15376-15000 = 376. So 149^10 ≡ 376.\n149^20 = 376^2 = 141376. 625*226 = 141250. 141376-141250 = 126. So 149^20 ≡ 126.\n149^25 = 149^20 * 149^5 = 126 * 124 = 15624. 625*24 = 15000. 15624-15000 = 624. So 149^25 ≡ 624 ≡ -1 mod 625.\n\nSo 149^25 ≡ -1 mod 625. Then 149^50 ≡ 1, 149^150 = (149^50)^3 ≡ 1. So 149^151 ≡ 149. Confirmed!\n\nGreat, so 149^151 ≡ 149 mod 625 is correct.\n\nNow let me verify 151^149 mod 625 = 476. Let me check via a different route. \n\n151^25 mod 625? Let me compute. 151^2 ≡ 301, 151^4 ≡ 601, 151^5 = 151^4*151 = 601*151 = 90751. 625*145 = 90625. 90751-90625 = 126. So 151^5 ≡ 126.\n151^10 = 126^2 = 15876. 625*25 = 15625. 15876-15625 = 251. So 151^10 ≡ 251.\n151^20 = 251^2 = 63001. 625*100 = 62500. 63001-62500 = 501. So 151^20 ≡ 501.\n151^25 = 151^20 * 151^5 = 501*126 = 63126. 625*101 = 63125. 63126-63125 = 1. So 151^25 ≡ 1 mod 625!\n\nSo 151^25 ≡ 1 mod 625. Then 151^149 = 151^(25*5 + 24) = 151^24 = (151^25)/151... no, 151^149 = 151^(125) * 151^24 = (151^25)^5 * 151^24 = 1 * 151^24.\n\n151^24 = 151^20 * 151^4 = 501 * 601 = 301101. 625*481 = 300625. 301101-300625 = 476. So 151^24 ≡ 476 mod 625. Confirmed!\n\nSo N mod 625 = 149 + 476 = 625 ≡ 0. Confirmed.\n\nNow N mod 16: Let me recheck.\n149 mod 16 = 5. 5^151 mod 16. Order of 5 mod 16 is 4 (5,9,13,1). 151 mod 4 = 3. 5^3 = 125 ≡ 13 mod 16. ✓\n151 mod 16 = 7. 7^149 mod 16. Order of 7 mod 16 is 2 (7,1). 149 mod 2 = 1. 7^1 = 7. ✓\nN mod 16 = 13+7 = 20 ≡ 4. ✓\n\nN mod 10000 = 2500. ✓\n\nN mod 149 = 2: 149^151 ≡ 0, 151^149 ≡ 2^149 mod 149. 2^148 ≡ 1 (Fermat, 149 prime). 2^149 ≡ 2. ✓\nN mod 151 = 149: 151^149 ≡ 0, 149^151 ≡ (-2)^151 = -2^151 mod 151. 2^150 ≡ 1. 2^151 ≡ 2. -2 mod 151 = 149. ✓\n\nCRT mod 22499: x ≡ 2 mod 149, x ≡ 149 mod 151. x = 300. ✓\n\nCRT mod 224990000: x ≡ 300 mod 22499, x ≡ 2500 mod 10000.\nx = 300 + 22499s, 2499s ≡ 2200 mod 10000, s = 7800.\nx = 300 + 22499*7800 = 175492500.\nfloor(175492500/22499) = 7800 (since 22499*7800 = 175492200, remainder 300). ✓\n\nAnswer: 7800.\n\nLet me also sanity check that 22499*7800 = 175492200. 22499*7800 = 22499*78*100. 22499*78: 22499*78 = 22499*(80-2) = 1799920 - 44998 = 1754922. *100 = 175492200. ✓ And +300 = 175492500. ✓\n\nSo the last four digits are 7800.", "tool_calls": [], "created_at": 1787225677}
